A||||9||BENGAL4||OVERHEATING PLEASE HELP.
Z||000000||BENGAL4||08-02-99||06:38 AM||||I finally finished my timing set project. I also put a new waterpump on, since I had to remove the old one anyway, I checked for leaks and everything looked fine. I then took it out and it began to overheat. I got it cooled down, added some more coolant, and tried again. The temp gauge stayed @ 240 just below the red zone for awhile then it moved up to 260 and I shut it off. I checked for heat it all of the hoses and the only place the coolant was not hot was the overflow container in the front. Now when it overheated the first time, this is where I added water. I filled the container to the top. It seem that there is enough coolant in the system. This did not overheat before I made the changes. The only mods I made is a power pully and I added an AC belt. The AC had never worked since I've had the car. Please help. i sure it's something simple but I'm stumped.<p><br>Bill<br>
Z||000001||Cooter||08-02-99||06:48 AM||||I have never added/changed coolant in my Fiero (shame on me), but I know that there is a specific procedure for doing it. I have heard that you cant get enough water in the engine without following the procedure. Sounds like this may be the case.
Z||000002||FieroMike||08-02-99||10:04 AM||||1.) Check your thermostat, make sure it is opening.<br>2.) Check to make sure your fan is coming on.<br>3.) Make sure that there are no kinks where the lower hose meets the metal tube under the car. (I have seen Fieros that run over objects and it bends the pipe up, pinching it.<br>4.) Also make sure you filled it correctly.  <p>About the water, Anti-Freeze has a higher boiling point than water, so if there is a lot of water in the system it WILL make it run hotter. 
Z||000003||RICK||08-02-99||11:01 AM||||It sounds like you might have air in the system, if you get enough air in the system when the air pocket gets to the water pump it has nothing to pump. <br>Somebody correct me if I'm wrong but while fillinging the system don't you have to remove the front radiator cap to let the air excape, once it is full the front cap goes back on and you continue to fill the system.  Remove the thermostat while filling.
Z||000004||batboy||08-02-99||06:20 PM||||Rick is right, sounds like you have air in the cooling system. Whenever you have any of the radiator or heater hoses off and especially if you change the water pump, you must purge the air from the coolant system on a Fiero. This is critical, adding coolant to the overflow recovery tank will not, and I repeat, will not remove the air. With the engine cold, remove the radiator cap and the the thermostat housing cap and thermostat. Add coolant into the thermostat housing at the engine until coolant begins overflowing out of the radiator neck. Leave the thermostat out and put both caps on to the first notch, not to the second notch like you would normally run it. Start the car and let it idle until the temp gauge goes up past the 100 degree mark (don't let it get too hot). With the car still running, use a large rag and carefully remove the radiator cap. Watch out! The coolant is going to be extremely hot and sometimes air and/or pressure will cause coolant to burp up for a second like a geyser. With the car still running, slowly add more coolant to the radiator this time until the level is about at the bottom of the filler neck. Turn off the car and let it cool for a while. Replace the thermostat and both caps, click them both down to the second notch this time. Make sure coolant is at the proper level in the recovery tank, then start the car again and observe carefully that it doesn't overheat. Please note: coolant is an antifreeze and water mix, usually about a 50/50 ratio (do not run plain water only or just antifreeze only).
Z||000005||Linx||08-02-99||06:35 PM||||I had the same problem last month, after replacing my timing chain and gears too.  My engine overheated and alot of the water boiled away.  Broken down by the roadside, I attempted to pour more water into it, but it needed so much more that i was sure it was leaking, and that I had cracked something major; I had it towed home. The next day- I couldn't find a leak.  Then, I realized that my timing was off. Waaaaay off.  I'd eyeballed it with a light, but never bothered to put the ECM into service mode.  Duh!  Sure enough, in the service manual, one symptom for overheating is bad timing.
Z||000006||peabody||08-02-99||08:59 PM||||How do you put your ECM into service mode? We <br>just completed a 3.4 swap, using the fiero intake, dist, etc. Timed it with a light. Just doesn't have the "kick" you'd expect. Now with Lynk's comment about his ECM, is there some way I should be turning it on?
Z||000007||Stroker||08-02-99||09:20 PM||||peabody,to get the ECM into "service mode" just take the blackcover off of the console,where the cigarette lighter is,(held on by two torx screws) and ground the terminals by using either a wire(or paperclip)or a Sunpro or similar code scanner, THEN you can set your base timing.<br>Check the Chiltons or Haynes manuals for the procedure in detail. S.
Z||000008||BENGAL4||08-03-99||06:37 AM||||Thanks Rick & everyone for the help. When I refilled with coolant after the repairs, I neglected to read about the "filling procedure". I filled it again to Ricks & Haynes instructions, and now the temp stays @ 210-215 degrees. Now I know. After that was done, I finished tuning it up. I tried the new Bosch Platinum +4 plugs, new cap, rotor, wires and PCV valve. Finished it up with a ADS Superchip. The car runs great. Now its on to the interior. Thanks again.
Z||000009||SkinnyJ||08-04-99||12:14 AM||||I SUFFERED FROM THE SAME PROBLEM.SOUNDS LIKE AN AIR POCKET TO ME. JACK THE CAR UP IN THE BACK AND REMOVE THEMOSTAT.MAKE SURE THERMOSTAT HOUSING IS THE HIGHEST POINT ON THE VEHICLE AND ADD 50/50 MIX.WHEN FULL, LET SIT AND REFILL AS NEEDED.IT ALSO HELPS TO RUN THE ENGINE(WITH THERMO CAP INSTALLED) FOR A FEW MINUTES, THEN REPEAT STEPS ABOVE.
